00:03:12,413 --> 00:03:18,413
50 Greatest Harry Potter Moments is a documentary film directed by None.

2
00:06:24,827 --> 00:06:30,827
To mark the release two weeks ago of the eighth and final movie in the series, Robbie
Coltrane narrates a countdown of the movie franchise's best moments.

3
00:09:37,241 --> 00:09:43,241
From Harry's first meeting with Ron and Hermione aboard the
Hogwarts Express through to magical mysteries.

4
00:12:49,655 --> 00:12:55,655
Narrator (voice), played by Robbie Coltrane.

5
00:16:02,068 --> 00:16:08,068
Harry Potter (archive footage), played by Daniel Radcliffe.

6
00:19:14,482 --> 00:19:20,482
Hermione Granger (archive footage), played by Emma Watson.

7
00:22:26,896 --> 00:22:32,896
Ron Weasley (archive footage), played by Rupert Grint.

8
00:25:39,310 --> 00:25:45,310
Molly Weasley (archive footage), played by Julie Walters.

9
00:28:51,724 --> 00:28:57,724
Arthur Weasley (archive footage), played by Mark Williams.

10
00:32:04,137 --> 00:32:10,137
Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban is a 2004 fantasy film
directed by Alfonso Cuarón from a screenplay by Steve Kloves.

11
00:35:16,551 --> 00:35:22,551
It is based on the 1999 novel Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban by J.

12
00:38:28,965 --> 00:38:34,965
It is the sequel to Harry Potter and the Chamber of Secrets (2002) and
the third instalment in the Harry Potter film series.

13
00:41:41,379 --> 00:41:47,379
The film stars Daniel Radcliffe as Harry Potter, alongside Rupert Grint and Emma Watson
as Harry's best friends Ron Weasley and Hermione Granger, respectively.

14
00:44:53,793 --> 00:44:59,793
It chronicles Harry's third year at Hogwarts and his quest to uncover the truth about his past, including the
connection recently escaped Azkaban prisoner Sirius Black has to Harry and his deceased parents.

15
00:48:06,206 --> 00:48:12,206
With this film, the Harry Potter series switched to a
longer eighteen-month production cycle.

16
00:51:18,620 --> 00:51:24,620
Cuarón was selected as director from a list that
included Callie Khouri and Kenneth Branagh.

17
00:54:31,034 --> 00:54:37,034
The cast of previous instalments returned for the film, with the additions of Gary
Oldman, David Thewlis, Timothy Spall, and Emma Thompson, among others.

18
00:57:43,448 --> 00:57:49,448
It was the first appearance of Michael Gambon as Professor Albus
Dumbledore, due to Richard Harris's death in 2002.

19
01:00:55,862 --> 01:01:01,862
Principal photography began in February 2003 at Leavesden Film Studios.

20
01:04:08,275 --> 01:04:14,275
It was the first in the series to extensively use real-life locations,
with sets built in Scotland and scenes shot in London.

21
01:07:20,689 --> 01:07:26,689
Filming wrapped in November 2003.

22
01:10:33,103 --> 01:10:39,103
The film was released on 31 May 2004 in the United
Kingdom, and on 4 June 2004 in North America.

23
01:13:45,517 --> 01:13:51,517
It was the first Harry Potter film to use IMAX
Technology and was released in IMAX theaters.

24
01:16:57,931 --> 01:17:03,931
Prisoner of Azkaban grossed a total of $808 million worldwide, making it
the second-highest-grossing film of 2004, behind Shrek 2.

25
01:20:10,344 --> 01:20:16,344
The film received critical acclaim, with particular praise for
Cuarón's direction and the lead actors' performances.

26
01:23:22,758 --> 01:23:28,758
It is credited for marking a notable shift in the franchise's tone and directing style and
is often regarded by critics and fans alike as the best Harry Potter film.

27
01:26:35,172 --> 01:26:41,172
It was nominated for two Academy Awards, Best Original Music Score and
Best Visual Effects, at the 77th Academy Awards in 2004.

28
01:29:47,586 --> 01:29:53,586
It was followed by Harry Potter and the Goblet of Fire in 2005.
